# Test: an interface leaf-list is an OR, one nftables rule per named interface.
# VALIDATES: spec-policyroute-interface-list-matches-no-packet AC-1, AC-4, AC-6 --
#            a policy naming eth0 and l2tp* installs TWO rules, one comparing the
#            full 16-byte name eth0 and one comparing the 4-byte prefix l2tp,
#            each setting the one fwmark the single `table 100` rule allocated.
# PREVENTS: the two interface matches returning to one rule. nftables ANDs the
#           matches inside a rule, so one rule asking for both names matches no
#           packet: the policy never fires and the traffic follows the main table,
#           with no log line and no commit error to say so.
#
# The DISCRIMINATING assertions are the two reject lines. A whole-table dump
# cannot tell an AND from an OR, because two matches in one rule print the same
# substrings as the same two matches in two rules. The fixture prints one RULE
# line per programmed rule for exactly that reason (policyRuleDump,
# internal/test/fixture/netfilter_fixture_policy.go), and a rejection on a single
# line carrying both names is what separates the two shapes.
#
# Neither interface has to exist. An iifname match compares packet metadata
# rather than looking an index up, so no option=netns-link is declared and this
# test stays out of the netns-only population.
#
# Requires CAP_NET_ADMIN.
#
# ISOLATION: read the header of policy-boot-apply.ci. Every test in test/policy/
# writes the same `inet ze_pr` table and the same global ip-rule table, so the
# suite runs serial with a per-test namespace. Do NOT raise its parallelism.

option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
firewall {
	backend nft;
}
policy {
	route steer {
		interface eth0;
		interface "l2tp*";
		rule webmark {
			from {
				destination-port 80,443;
				protocol tcp;
			}
			then {
				table 100;
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# The key is DOTTED: the engine maps the registry name "policy-routes" to the
# subsystem "policy.routes" (CanonicalSubsystemName), so ze.log.policy-routes
# matches nothing. policy-boot-apply.ci carries the full explanation.
option=env:var=ze.log.policy.routes:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.firewall:value=info

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture policy/policy-interface-list:timeout=10s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=policy routes applied
expect=stdout:contains=table inet ze_pr
expect=stdout:pattern=RULE .*iifname "eth0".*meta mark set
expect=stdout:pattern=RULE .*iifname "l2tp\*".*meta mark set
reject=stdout:pattern=RULE .*eth0.*l2tp
reject=stdout:pattern=RULE .*l2tp.*eth0
